# LEMMAS ON INFINITE MEMBERS: points whose only feasible route to the
# answer rewrites with a lemma on an infinite member of a recursive,
# parametric type.
#
# A type system without lemmas on infinite members is not a type system,
# so these points ask for them, for example map id s = s on an infinite
# stream. Assertions are combinators, so they accept parameters used as
# type parameters.
#
# THE PROGRAMS. A stream is a Scott list with no nil, as in
# `contest_clauses.pl`. The main points read one stream, an infinite
# member of the stream type whose element type is the Booleans:
#
#     s = Y (\s. cons b0 (cons b1 ... (cons b301 s)))
#
# with b_i true when 3 divides i and false otherwise. Its period, 302, is
# larger than every index read, so no two of the tails read are equal
# terms. The stream carries no state in a seed: every layer is the same
# fixpoint (2026-10-02: a stream whose layers are derivable
# at an opaque seed does not depend on whether hypotheses are admitted in
# the replay, while `from n = n : from (succ n)` would). Each main point
# reads one element of s through K layers of `map id`:
#
#     nth N (map id (map id ... (map id s)))    K layers
#
# - infinite_stream_map_identity_layers: N = 300, K = 300, the layers
#   written out; b_300, true.
# - infinite_stream_map_identity_counted: the same layers built at run
#   time by `stack k s = k s (\p. map (\x. x) (stack p s))` from the
#   numeral K, the identity spelled inline; N = 301, b_301, false.
# - infinite_stream_map_identity_shifted: N = 299, K = 300, the layers
#   written out; b_299, false.
#
# THE INTENDED ANSWER IS RIGHT UNDER DEFINITION 14. Each program P reduces by
# beta to the Boolean B written above: nth N walks N tails, the tail of
# `map id x` is `map id (tail x)` once x has its weak head, and the element
# reached is b_N under K identities. So ⊢_β P = B, and P ≃_O B by the soundness
# of the derivable equations for Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): B is an
# answer, P ⊑_O B, and no other closed βη normal form is one (Böhm's theorem). The scored claim is that Boolean and the trap
# the other, the finite head class of `contest_hard_claims.pl` (the
# trap's equation is refuted by Boehm's theorem).
#
# WHY ONLY A LEMMA REACHES IT IN TIME. The element at index N of the
# outer layer needs the weak head of every tail `map id^j (tail_i s)`,
# j up to K and i up to N: the tail of `map id x` is taken only once x
# has a weak head. These N times K terms are pairwise distinct, since the
# period exceeds N, so no table and no sharing merges two of them. A
# finite observation of the stream under the layers, with a premise on
# the stream, such as `nth n (map id x) = nth n x` for a numeral n and
# an infinite stream x, or nth commuted with map, used K times, leaves
# `nth N s`, N steps. Each is a lemma on an infinite stream, whatever its
# element type; the type parameter is the element type. The
# point writes neither the lemma nor its premise nor its proof: the
# setting chooses them.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-02 on CPUs 0 to 5 through doc/contest_limited.sh,
# N = K = 300, period 302. The main points keep the default limits, 1 s,
# 32 MB of stack and 32 MB of table space: they are beyond points.
# - Unfolding, the baseline tabling(weak_head) at 60 s and 1024 MB reads
#   every main point's answer in 9589 to 10662 ms with 256 to 258 MB of
#   table space (corpora/contest_hard_baseline.pl).
# - At the points' own limits every reference setting and every setting of
#   corpora/contest_entries.pl misses all three main points: each stops
#   at about 1 s or exhausts its table space.
# - The program after the lemma, `nth N s`, takes tabling(weak_head) 90
#   to 114 ms at 1 s, 32 MB and 32 MB, three readings for each N.
# - Every setting reads the three non-stream points at their limits.
#
# TRAPS FOR A LEMMA WITHOUT ITS PREMISE. `nth n (map id x) = nth n x`
# and `nth n (map f x) = f (nth n x)` fail on a term x that is not a
# stream, and rewriting `map id x` to x answers the trap of
# infinite_stream_map_identity_non_stream_case.
# The two non-stream points apply map id to J = \n. \c. c H T Z, a cons
# cell with a third field: `map id J` takes J's cons branch, rebuilds
# `cons H (map id T)` and applies it to Z, so the result is
# `\c. c H (map id T)`: Z has taken the nil handler's place, and every
# later argument moves one place left.
# - infinite_stream_map_identity_non_stream_case: `map id J` applied to a
#   nil handler `\a. \b. \c. true` and a cons handler `\h. \t. \z. false`:
#   the nil handler lands in the cons handler's place and answers true.
#   With J in place of `map id J` the cons handler answers false.
# - infinite_stream_map_identity_non_stream_nth: `nth 0 (map id J)` with
#   H = \g. g true false, T = 0 and Z = false: nth's index 0 lands in
#   the cons handler's place and selects H, which is applied to nth's
#   cons handler G, and `G true false` reduces to `0 true ...`, true.
#   Each of the two lemmas, used without its premise, gives `nth 0 J`
#   (the second as `id (nth 0 J)`): G receives H as the head, 0
#   selects it, and `H Z` is `false true false`, false.
# - infinite_stream_map_identity_premised_non_stream: a term that passes
#   the premise `map id X = X` and is not a stream, after the witness
#   against the premise `T @ X = X`.
#   X = \n. \c. c H T E (W W), W = \w. \h. \m. \n. \c. c H T E (w w),
#   with H = \a. \b. true, T = E = 0. `map f X` takes X's cons branch,
#   rebuilds `cons (f H) (map f T)` and applies it to E and W W: E takes
#   the nil handler's place, and W W, in the cons handler's, discards
#   both fields and answers X. So `map f X` reduces to X for every f,
#   and `map id X = X` holds by beta. `nth 0 (map (\x. false) X)` is
#   then `nth 0 X`, which selects H and applies it to E and W W, true.
#   Commuting nth with map under the premise gives
#   `(\x. false) (nth 0 X)`, false.
# The intended answers are finite heads by the same beta argument, with
# the other Boolean the trap. All three reductions were checked by
# normalisation.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - proofs by coinduction on streams, the bisimulation that a lemma on an
#   infinite member rests on: J. J. M. M. Rutten, Universal Coalgebra: A
#   Theory of Systems, TCS 2000, doi:10.1016/S0304-3975(00)00056-6; D.
#   Kozen, A. Silva, Practical Coinduction, Mathematical Structures in
#   Computer Science 2016, doi:10.1017/S0960129515000493.
# - removing the intermediate structure `map id` builds, by a law instead
#   of by evaluation: P. Wadler, Deforestation: Transforming Programs to
#   Eliminate Trees, TCS 1990, doi:10.1016/0304-3975(90)90147-A.

